---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { site } from "../site";
---

<Base
  title={"About and editorial policy | " + site.name}
  description="AI Agent Governance helps teams decide what an agent may do, who owns the decision and what evidence to keep."
>
  <section class="page-intro wrap narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">About this site</p>
    <h1>A handbook for governing AI agents.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      AI Agent Governance helps teams decide what an agent may do, who owns the
      decision and what evidence to keep.
    </p>
  </section>
  <div class="prose wrap narrow">
    <h2>From a policy to an operating decision</h2>
    <p>
      Giving an agent a tool raises concrete questions. Who owns its deployment?
      Which systems may it reach? What needs approval? Who can stop it when the
      scope changes? These guides turn those questions into records, checks and
      procedures a team can use.
    </p>
    <p>
      Start with the{" "}
      <a href="/guides/ai-agent-governance-framework/">governance framework</a>,
      build an <a href="/guides/ai-agent-inventory/">agent inventory</a> and
      review <a href="/guides/ai-agent-access-control/">access controls</a>. The{" "}
      <a href="/tools/decision-record/">decision tool</a> helps document one
      deployment. The <a href="/resources/">resource library</a> has templates
      for reviews, incidents and ongoing ownership.
    </p>
    <h2>Keep decisions tied to evidence</h2>
    <p>
      The examples distinguish a policy statement from an enforced permission
      and a documented feature from an observed result. A useful decision record
      names the permitted work, the person responsible and the evidence behind
      approval. It also says what would trigger another review.
    </p>
    <p>
      Templates are engineering starting points. Requirements for a particular
      industry, contract or jurisdiction need the appropriate specialist review.
    </p>
    <h2>Sources and review dates</h2>
    <p>
      Technical claims link to primary sources: standards, project documentation
      or the code that implements a behavior. Each guide has a review date, a
      source list and a downloadable Markdown version. Definitions and examples
      can be cited using the citation at the end of the article.
    </p>
    <p>
      A review date records a content check. Product trials and deployment tests
      need their own evidence. Examples say whether they are conceptual,
      runnable or browser-only demonstrations; fictional records are labeled.
    </p>
    <h2>Product examples and ownership</h2>
    <p>
      A product belongs in a guide when its documented behavior helps explain
      the topic. Comparisons distinguish what the documentation says from what a
      reader still needs to test. Instructions and templates should remain
      useful with other implementations.
    </p>
    <p>
      This publication shares ownership with Emisar, Ryker and co:op. Relevant
      examples disclose that relationship beside the mention.
    </p>
    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      The interactive tools run in your browser. They do not send the values you
      enter to us. We do not add analytics, advertising scripts or tracking
      cookies. Cloudflare Pages serves the site and may process request data
      under{" "}
      <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">
        Cloudflare's privacy policy
      </a>
      .
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
